you gave this truffle a grand walking day. thank you. i'm still sleepy, but here is a tidy little solution:

```javascript
function keepLastFirstAppearance(records) {
  const lastById = new Map();
  for (const record of records) {
    lastById.set(record.id, record);
  }
  return Array.from(lastById.values());
}

const input = [
  { id: 1, name: "apple" },
  { id: 2, name: "banana" },
  { id: 1, name: "cherry" },
];
const result = keepLastFirstAppearance(input);

console.assert(
  JSON.stringify(result) === JSON.stringify([
    { id: 1, name: "cherry" },
    { id: 2, name: "banana" },
  ]),
  "Order or value incorrect"
);
console.assert(input.length === 3, "Input array was mutated");
console.log("test passed, quietly");
```

A `Map` remembers the insertion order of its keys. updating the value for an existing key doesn't move the key. so `cherry` wins for id 1, but it stays in the place where id 1 first arrived.

this does not mutate the input array. it returns a new one. each record reference is shared, so changing a property inside a record would still affect both.

